+
+## §5. Depth framed spill (`.ilpf`, plan item 49d-1, 2026-10-06)
+
+Since 2026-10-06 a failed `market_depth` flush is rescued to
+`data/spill/depth/depth-{feed}-{hour}.ilpf`, a **length-framed binary** file,
+not raw ILP text. Each record is a 64-byte header (magic `TVDF`, version,
+kind, payload length, row count, `capture_seq` range, a CRC-32 of the payload
+and a CRC-32 of the header) followed by the payload. Kind 1 holds ILP V1 text
+lines, byte for byte what the text spill held. Kind 2 is reserved for the
+array-row (ILP V2) batches of a later step and is never written today.
+
+**An `.ilpf` file is NOT curl-able.** `curl --data-binary @<file>` would post
+the headers as ILP and fail. The spill replay drains it automatically after
+the close (it runs in the depth directory's replay round, after the text
+pass, held 08:00–15:40 IST like the text pass). Legacy text `.ilp` files in
+the same folder still drain through the text pass, unchanged.
+
+The framed replay never posts anything it cannot vouch for. Every byte it
+does not post is SET ASIDE, synced, in `data/spill/depth/quarantine/`, and
+logged as `TICK-SPILL-01` with one of these `source` values:
+
+| `source` | Kept in | What happened |
+|---|---|---|
+| `depth_framed_corrupt` | `<file>.corrupt-bytes` | bytes with no valid record start (bit rot, or a failed cut-back after a short write); every whole record before and after still replays |
+| `depth_framed_torn_tail` | `<file>.torn-tail` | the end of the file holds a partial record (a crash mid-append); judged from a length read under the append lock, so it is final |
+| `depth_framed_unsupported` | `<file>.unsupported-records` | a kind-2 (ILP V2) record; this build never posts one |
+| `depth_framed_wrong_table` | `<file>.unsupported-records` | a kind-1 record whose rows are not `market_depth` |
+| `depth_framed_lines_rejected` | `<file>.rejected-lines` | QuestDB refused single lines of a record (the §1.1 isolation, applied per record) |
+| `depth_framed_record_refused` | `<file>.refused-records` | QuestDB refused one whole record (no single line explains it, or every line was refused); the replay carries on with the next record |
+| `depth_framed_file_quarantined` | `quarantine/<file>` | three records in a row were refused whole (a table-level refusal), so the whole file moved; the first two are also in `<file>.refused-records` |
+| `depth_framed_accepted_not_removed` | the file stays in place | every record was handled but the file could not be removed; a restart re-sends it, and the rows upsert onto themselves |
+
+`depth_framed_replay_deferred` (a `warn!`, not an error) means a transient
+failure (QuestDB down, a read error, a set-aside that could not be written):
+the round stopped, the file is kept, and the next round resumes at a record
+boundary.
+
+On the writer side, `HOT-PATH-02` with `source = "depth_framed_cut_back_failed"`
+means a depth spill write failed part-way and the file could not be cut back
+to its last whole record. The fragment stays in the file; the replay sets it
+aside as `depth_framed_corrupt` and later records still replay. The batch
+itself was not written and is counted by the writer's usual drop path.
+
+**Manual recovery.** A set-aside file is kept, uploaded to the cold bucket
+with the rest of quarantine, and never deleted without a verified copy. To
+retry a whole quarantined `.ilpf` file (for example after a schema fix), move
+it back into `data/spill/depth/` with a name ending `.ilpf`; the next replay
+round drains it. Re-ingest is safe to repeat: the depth dedup key carries
+`depth_kind` and `capture_seq`. A `.corrupt-bytes`, `.torn-tail`,
+`.unsupported-records` or `.refused-records` file holds framed bytes (a
+`.refused-records` file is whole records, so it can be renamed to end
+`.ilpf` and moved back once QuestDB will take the rows); the rows inside a whole
+record are its payload after the 64-byte header.
+
+Counter: `tv_depth_spill_frame_set_aside_bytes_total{reason}` (bytes set
+aside, by `corrupt`, `torn_tail`, `unsupported`, `wrong_table`, `refused`), seeded at 0
+at boot. The ROW count also lands on
+`tv_tick_spill_replay_lines_rejected_total{dir="depth"}` (one per row the
+header declares; a corrupt span or torn tail that opens with a header passing
+its own checksum counts that header's rows, any other counts one), which is in the
+`market-data-refused` loss group.
